{"type": "text", "text": "I have all the information needed. Here are my findings.

## Summary

The bug is in the TUI chat widget's user-turn construction. When a user submits a message, the permission profile is set to `None` for the external-sandbox variant while being preserved for every other sandbox policy variant.

## 1. Chat widget message-submission source file

`/workspace/repo/codex-rs/tui/src/chatwidget.rs`

The submission path is the function chain ending in `submit_user_message_with_history_and_shell_escape_policy` (starts at line 6179), which builds the user-turn op. Related entry points: `submit_user_message` (6146), `submit_user_message_with_history_record` (6153), `submit_user_message_with_shell_escape_policy` (6166).

The user-turn op constructor is in `/workspace/repo/codex-rs/tui/src/app_command.rs`, `AppCommand::user_turn` (lines 140-170), which wraps `Op::UserTurn { ... permission_profile, sandbox_policy, ... }`.

## 2. The suspect branch (the bug)

`/workspace/repo/codex-rs/tui/src/chatwidget.rs`, lines 6413-6434:

```rust
let permission_profile = if matches!(
    self.config.permissions.sandbox_policy.get(),
    SandboxPolicy::ExternalSandbox { .. }
) {
    None
} else {
    Some(self.config.permissions.permission_profile())
};
let op = AppCommand::user_turn(
    items,
    self.config.cwd.to_path_buf(),
    self.config.permissions.approval_policy.value(),
    self.config.permissions.sandbox_policy.get().clone(),
    permission_profile,
    effective_mode.model().to_string(),
    ...
);
```

This is the match-over-sandbox-policy-variants the task describes. It is a two-arm `matches!`: the `ExternalSandbox { .. }` arm yields `None`, and the catch-all `else` arm preserves the profile via `Some(self.config.permissions.permission_profile())`. So the external-sandbox variant drops the permission profile while all other variants keep it.

`self.config.permissions.permission_profile()` (defined in `/workspace/repo/codex-rs/core/src/config/mod.rs:226`) simply returns the canonical stored profile, and `PermissionProfile` can represent external sandbox correctly (`From<&SandboxPolicy> for FileSystemSandboxPolicy` maps `ExternalSandbox { .. } => FileSystemSandboxPolicy::external_sandbox()` at `/workspace/repo/codex-rs/protocol/src/permissions.rs:980`). So the `None` branch appears to be an unnecessary/incorrect special-case that discards the profile.

## 3. Terminology clarification ("external" vs "legacy external")

- The `SandboxPolicy` enum (the older/"legacy" sandbox representation) is defined in `/workspace/repo/codex-rs/protocol/src/protocol.rs:1033`. Its external variant is `SandboxPolicy::ExternalSandbox { network_access }` at lines 1049-1054. Note there is no separately-named `LegacyExternalSandbox` enum variant — `SandboxPolicy` as a whole is treated as the legacy form throughout (see the many `from_legacy_sandbox_policy` conversions).
- The newer canonical `PermissionProfile` / `FileSystemSandboxKind::ExternalSandbox` lives in `/workspace/repo/codex-rs/protocol/src/permissions.rs:135` and `/workspace/repo/codex-rs/protocol/src/models.rs`.

## 4. The reverse/mirror handling (for context)

In the `SessionConfigured` handler, same file `/workspace/repo/codex-rs/tui/src/chatwidget.rs`, lines 2368-2388, the inbound event reconstructs a profile from the legacy sandbox policy when `permission_profile` is `None`:

```rust
let permission_sync = match event.permission_profile.clone() {
    Some(permission_profile) => self.config.permissions
        .set_permission_profile(permission_profile, event.cwd.as_path()),
    None => self.config.permissions
        .set_legacy_sandbox_policy(event.sandbox_policy.clone(), event.cwd.as_path()),
};
...
let permission_profile = event.permission_profile.clone().unwrap_or_else(|| {
    codex_protocol::models::PermissionProfile::from_legacy_sandbox_policy(&event.sandbox_policy)
});
```

This shows the system expects to fall back to the legacy `sandbox_policy` field when `permission_profile` is `None`, which is consistent with the outbound code at 6413 deliberately sending `None` for external sandbox and relying on the `sandbox_policy` field — but it also means any information the `PermissionProfile` carries beyond what the legacy `SandboxPolicy::ExternalSandbox` encodes (e.g., readable/unreadable roots, network nuances) would be lost on that path.

No files were modified."}

